 aid for  development and I'm talking about  development not about saving lives and  not about humanitarian aid but about  development foreign aid for development  has become largely about the delivery of  money and things and that deliberately  delivery has been done far too rapidly  without enough homework and with a  tremendous amount of attention paid to  checking off boxes on a matrix that we  now at least in the u.s. called the  results framework did the community help  dig the pit latrine check yes for  community ownership was a local water  users Association form checked yes for  sustainability a second reason is that  our established aid system in my view  willfully ignores human nature most  particularly the fact that generally  people do not maintain things that they  received for free and more important the  third reason is that development aid  much of foreign aid has become an  industry an industry whose main purpose  in being is its own self perpetuation  there are thousands upon thousands  hundreds of thousands of people in the  north in the West like myself who are  employed in this industry and we don't  want to lose our jobs to justify that  self perpetuation in my view and my  experience the aid industry has not been  able to face its  lessons it has not been able to see all  the things that ought to have learned  over 50 60 years the principal one being  that development is not about money and  things it is about institutional change  and the complex interactions of  political economies social structure and  culture that inform those institutions  this level of complexity the current aid  establishment is not capable of taking  on board and so we are doomed in my view  to continually get it wrong who can get  it right well at this point after all  these years it has to be the countries  themselves it's really their  responsibility and our role in future if  we have one and we can answer that  question later it's certainly a  diminished one and we need to back off  let go and work ourselves out of a job  like Thomas you've described some bad  programs I can think of one the birthing  suite they've been provided by a donor  25 years earlier in a little village in  Ethiopia and they'd left and thought  right we got some modern kit here had he  ever been used to deliver a single baby  no why because there was no doctor no  Midwife in that community if you were  giving birth and your baby got into  difficulties you depended on someone  being able to put you on the back of a  motorbike to travel to the nearest  hospital now that is not an argument  against aid that's an argument against  doing things in the wrong way so that's  the first point I make secondly does aid  make a positive difference yes it does  because there are children in school  today because of the support of other  countries there are children who are not  dying of diseases that our children  don't die off because of vaccination  programs there are people who would have  died because they are HIV positive who  are not currently dead because we  developed anti retrovirals and we  provided a lot of finance the Global  Fund has raised a lot of finance to help  countries and I reflect on my experience  the first African country ever went to  was Malawi and at that time virtually no  one was on antiretroviral drugs that was  in 2002 and the vice-president just in  man away she was asking well what could  we do now that prevailing development  view at the time was nobody thinking  about anti retrovirals because until you  get a health service in place you can't  deliver them actually we were wrong he  was right I changed our policies the  Development Secretary when I went back  in 2006 there from memory there about  400,000 people alive on antiretroviral  drugs and Aid has played a part in  helping them now the progress we can see  more fewer kids not being in school  reduction in the proportion of people in  the world living below the absolute  poverty line reduction in the rate of  infection with malaria is partly a  result of development within countries  and partly a result of the assistance  and it's not aid cannot be the answer of  course not  because it aid was the answer if you  right a large enough check then you  could solve the problems of development  and poverty that is not the case  ultimately and I agree with you on this  Thomas it is about societies countries  developing them cells and that is about  the decisions you take for yourself not  having a a war is a really good idea if  you want to develop why did in my time  and why did Sierra Leone and Afghanistan  share the distinction of having the  highest rates of infant mortality in the  world well in part because of the  history of conflict allied with lack of  access to clean water and sanitation  which is the biggest killer of children  under the age of five apart from the  lack of care if they get into difficulty  just after they have been born it's  about having the rule of law good  governance good politics accountable  politicians all of the things that I  think our history teaches us helped to  make a difference the less spectacular  bits of aid for instance working to  improve the ability of a finance  ministry to actually shift the cash  they've got in their budget whether it's  cash the country itself has been able to  raise or budget support that's been  given by donors to actually get it out  so it could get spent on employing  doctors and nurses buying drugs building  schools buying tables and textbooks that  is less spectacular but in the end it  will make a difference people in eight  country a say show me how your aid is  making a difference I've paid the taxes  actually working together to cooperate  to give financial support to a ministry  that wants more kids into school but  lacks the cash is the most sensible  thing to do but it doesn't quite square  with donor country's desire to say well  there goes the British power ah that's  the vaccine it paid for oh that's the  kid that we are now supporting so we  have to work our way through that and  that is partly down to you so it's about  the right kind of aid in the right way  there are tensions and I'm sure we'll  discuss them about accountability to  donors because at the end politicians in  any country should be accountable to  their people not to me when I was the  international development secretary it's  not my job don't come and talk to me go  give your own government  hard time just as you managed to do to  us here in the UK and lastly it's both a  moral Crescent for me it's also about  our self-interest in the true sense of  the word not selfishness because in the  end a country a world in which there's  great gaps in poverty equality and  opportunity will not be a stable and  secure world and why do so many people  get in leaky boats and some of them  drown in order to try and get to Europe  from the coast of North Africa it's  because the life that they could see for  themselves where they live at the moment  isn't going to offer them something  better and they want to move as human  beings have always done to somewhere  else in the world where they can have a  better life and that is a really good  argument for priding help to support  people who in the end have to do it for  themselves  [Applause]
 

